LES\t5\nA. Writing Parts of an Article\t5\nTitle\t5\nByline, ORCID, and Membership Citation\t5\nORCID\t6\nIEEE Membership Grades\t6\nConsortia and group authorship\t6\nFirst Footnotes\t6\nFirst Paragraph:\t7\nHuman/Animal Research\t8\nSecond Paragraph:\t8\nThird Paragraph:\t10\nB. The Body of the Article\t10\nAbstract\t10\nIndex Terms\t10\nNote to Practitioners\t11\nNomenclature\t11\nText Section Headings\t11\nIntroduction\t12\nText Equations\t13\nAppendix\t13\nAcknowledgment\t13\nReferences\t14\nText Citation of Figures and Tables\t14\nBiographies\t16\nC. Other Text\t17\nInclusive Language\t17\nFootnotes\t17\nLists in Text\t17\nDedication Line(s)\t18\nNote Added in Proof\t19\nD. Other Types of Papers\t19\nEditorials\t19\nBrief Papers\t19\nShort Papers, Letters, Correspondence, and Communications\t20\nComments and Replies\t20\nCorrections/Errata/Addendums\t20\nBook Reviews\t21\nObituaries/In Memoriam\t21\nE. Writing Style for Transactions\t21\nAcronyms\t21\nSpelling\t22\nTrademarks\t22\nPlurals\t22\nHyphenation Rules\t22\nThe En or Em Dash\t22\nGrammar\t23\nContractions\t23\nCapitalization\t23\nDates\t23\nPercentages and Decimals\t24\nRanges With Units\t24\nMath\t24\nEquation Numbers\t25\nDisplayed Equations\t25\nF. General Layout Rules\t25\nIII. GRAMMAR AND USAGE IN TRANSACTIONS\t26\nA. Rules of Grammar\t26\nIV. APPENDIX\t28\nA. Some Common Acronyms and Abbreviations\t28\nB. Common Hyphenations and Misspellings\t32\nC. Table of Units and Quantity Symbols\t35\nD. Miscellaneous Alphabetical Abbreviations, Acronyms, and Symbols\t43\nE. Inclusive Language Guide\t63\n\f\nI. INTRODUCTION\nA. Purpose of Manual\nThis style manual provides general writing guidelines for IEEE Transactions, Journals, and Letters. For guidance in grammar and usage not included in this manual, please consult  The Chicago Manual of Style, published by the University of Chicago Press.\nB. Definition of a Transactions and Explanation of the Review Process\nAll IEEE Transactions are refereed archival journals. This means that each Transactions has a volunteer Editor or Editor-in-Chief (EIC) who is responsible for soliciting manuscripts and overseeing the peer review and revision process for the journal. The referees (at least two, according to IEEE policy), together with the Editor and sometimes with volunteer Associate Editors, determine the technical merit of each submitted article and make a recommendation to accept, accept with revision, or reject it.\nOnce an author has made any necessary changes and an article has been accepted in final form for publication, and the judgment and revision based on technical merit are complete, the articles are sent to the IEEE Transactions/Journals Department for publication in the Transactions.\nC. IEEE Transactions Editing Philosophy\nThe IEEE’s responsibility in editing articles for the Transactions is not to do any editing of the technical content, but is instead to render the work as readable, grammatically correct, and as consistent with IEEE style as possible.\nSince we are concerned with style mainly in the sense of IEEE house style, we do not try to change an author’s style of writing. We do a mechanical edit to correct or question grammatical errors, obvious inconsistencies or omissions, spelling, and punctuation. Since we work with highly technical text, we also do extensive formatting of mathematical material.\nSome manuscripts require closer editing than others; for example, some are from authors unfamiliar with the English language. Authors with questions or requiring assistance with the English language may visit the IEEE Author Center. Often, an IEEE Staff Editor must determine how to correct a grammatical error or decide what can be safely changed or corrected without altering the author’s original meaning. Because of the highly technical nature of the material we deal with, and because of our often limited understanding of that material, it is especially important that Staff Editors do not risk making any unnecessary changes or any that may affect the author’s meaning.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"document","si":0,"ei":0,"sm":{"ds_uephf":true,"ds_ufphf":true,"ds_lhs":1,"ds_fi":"kix.hf3","ds_ulhfl":false,"ds_hi":"kix.hf1","ds_epfi":"kix.hf2","ds_ephi":"kix.hf0","ds_fpfi":"kix.syjpi9z4g7yo","ds_fphi":"kix.svozahvwnr42"}},{"ty":"ae","et":"list","id":"kix.list.100","epm":{"le_nb":{"nl_4":{"b_gf":"%4.","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":true,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":false,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Arial","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400},"b_ifl":162.0,"b_gs":"","b_a":0,"b_sn":1,"b_gt":13,"b_il":180.0},"nl_3":{"b_gf":"%3.","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":true,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":false,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Arial","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400},"b_ifl":126.0,"b_gs":"","b_a":0,"b_sn":1,"b_gt":10,"b_il":144.0},"nl_2":{"b_gf":"%2.","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":true,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":false,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Arial","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400},"b_ifl":99.0,"b_gs":"","b_a":2,"b_sn":1,"b_gt":15,"b_il":108.0},"nl_1":{"b_gf":"%1.","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":true,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_b